            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The consensus bifurcates 'bow' into two distinct symbols based on context: the meteorological rainbow representing God's covenant and mercy, and the weapon representing military power, warfare, and strength (where breaking the bow signifies the defeat of that power).\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The book unifies the symbol, defining the bow as a kingdom's covenant and the power joined by it, where breaking the bow means unstringing the kingdom and ending its might.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Covenant/mercy (rainbow) and military power/strength (weapon).\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"A kingdom's covenant and the power joined by it.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"COMPATIBLE_OVERLAP\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The consensus restricts the 'covenant' meaning to the meteorological rainbow (Genesis 9) and 'power' to the weapon, treating them as related but distinct symbolic referents. The book merges them, applying both 'covenant' and 'power' to the symbol as a whole, so that a kingdom's weapon-bow also represents its covenant.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"There is no direct contradiction of the core elements (covenant and might), but the book rejects the consensus bifurcation, synthesizing the two meanings into a single overarching concept.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book takes the two distinct consensus meanings (covenant and power) and synthesizes them into a single definition. By interpreting the weapon as the power joined by a kingdom's covenant, the book extends the 'covenant' association beyond just the rainbow, thereby refining the boundary of the symbol.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"While Genesis 9 explicitly links the rainbow to a covenant, and passages like Jeremiah 49 link the weapon-bow to a kingdom's might, applying the concept of 'covenant' to every mention of a literal weapon-bow may over-read poetic descriptions of military strength.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
